# Git sees a CSV as plain text and knows nothing about quoted fields, so an
# end-of-line conversion rewrites the data itself rather than just the file's
# line terminators: the content column holds e-mail bodies with newlines inside
# quotes, and a Windows checkout under `text=auto` turns each of those into a
# CRLF. Store the generated data and the cached e-mails byte for byte instead,
# so every clone reads back exactly what the build wrote.
#
# Line endings inside e-mail bodies are normalised where they belong, when the
# body is parsed -- see massmail_read_email() in data-raw/01-setup-mass-mail.R.
# One normaliser, in the build, rather than a second hidden one in git.
data/massmail_data.csv          -text
data/massmail_data.rda          binary
data-raw/unparsed-emails.csv    -text
data-raw/massmail-emails/*.html -text
README_files/**/*.png           binary

# Everything else is ordinary source, checked out with LF everywhere so the
# build and the tests see the same bytes on any machine.
*.R             text eol=lf
*.qmd           text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.Rproj         text eol=lf
.gitattributes  text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf
.Rbuildignore   text eol=lf
